# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
  "base": [
    ("What a Flatwoods Lot Means for the Subgrade",
     "<p>Much of Greater Orlando and the Suncoast sits on flatwoods soil series like Myakka, Smyrna, Immokalee, EauGallie and Pomona, which hold a water table within 18 inches of the surface for one to four months most years. That is workable ground for a driveway, but only if we test it before pouring instead of assuming it behaves like the lot next door. Wet, weak native soil will not compact to a stable subgrade no matter how long a plate compactor runs over it. On a {city} property with this kind of soil, we remove the saturated layer and build up with compacted fill rather than forming directly on soggy ground, which is what keeps the slab from settling unevenly within a few years.</p>"),
    ("Ridge Sand Doesn't Behave Like Flatwoods Soil",
     "<p>The sand ridge soils west of Orlando, Candler, Astatula and Tavares, drain almost too well: the water table can sit more than 80 inches down. That sounds like easier ground to build on, but loose, excessively drained sand does not hold a compactor's pass the way a firmer soil does, and it can shift under the plate instead of locking together. On a ridge lot in {city}, we wet the material enough to bind it, then compact in shallow lifts rather than one deep pass. Skip that step and the driveway can still settle later, just for the opposite reason a flatwoods lot would.</p>"),
    ("Why Old Sod and Roots Cannot Stay Under a New Driveway",
     "<p>Florida's residential code allows clean sand or gravel fill up to 24 inches deep under a slab, or earth fill up to 8 inches, topped with at least 4 inches of clean graded base material. None of that is meant to sit on grass, roots or buried organic debris. If a {city} lot has old sod, mulch or a stump left in place, that material keeps decomposing after the concrete goes down, and the void it leaves is exactly where a slab cracks or dips first. Stripping the organic layer before fill goes in costs a step up front and is the difference between a flat driveway and one that settles in patches.</p>"),
    ("Grading the Driveway Away From the Garage Slab",
     "<p>A driveway that slopes toward the garage instead of away from it sends every afternoon storm straight at the house. Florida code requires fill to be compacted for uniform support, and part of getting that right is shaping the subgrade so water sheets off the surface and away from the foundation rather than pooling at the threshold. On a flat {city} lot, that sometimes means building the far edge of the driveway up slightly more than the garage end, even when the difference is not visible to the eye. It matters most on flatwoods soils that already hold water close to the surface for months at a time.</p>"),
    ("Working a Driveway Around a Protected Oak's Roots",
     "<p>Live oak roots can run well past the canopy and lift a slab years after it was poured. Orlando and Winter Park both protect trees at 6 inches in diameter at breast height by ordinance, and removing or cutting into the root zone of a protected tree needs a permit, not just a shovel. When we find roots in the base of a {city} driveway, we route the subgrade around them, or bridge over them with extra fill and reinforcement, instead of cutting whatever is in the way. Whether a protection ordinance applies depends on {city}'s own code, so it is worth checking before the first scoop of dirt moves.</p>"),
    ("When a Sandy Lot Still Has Clay Pockets",
     "<p>Not every sandy Florida lot is sand all the way down. Soils like Pomona and Felda look like typical fine sand at the surface, but a few feet down they change into a sandy clay loam or sandy clay layer that drains and compacts differently than the material above it. Grading a {city} driveway base, we expect that kind of shift rather than treating the whole excavation as one uniform material, because a compactor set up for loose sand will not do the same job on a denser clay pocket. Missing that change is one of the quieter reasons a base looks fine at pour time and still settles unevenly a year later.</p>"),
    ("Compacted for Uniform Support, Not Just Leveled by Eye",
     "<p>Florida's residential code does not just require fill under a slab, it requires that fill be compacted for uniform support, a different standard than simply backfilling and raking a surface flat. A base that looks even on the surface can still have soft spots underneath if it was not compacted in lifts and checked as the work went. For a {city} driveway, that means testing the subgrade rather than trusting that a few passes with a plate compactor did the job everywhere. A slab poured over an unevenly compacted base tends to crack along the soft spots first, usually well before it cracks anywhere else.</p>"),
    ("Why the Water Table Can Change Across One Driveway",
     "<p>Groundwater in Central Florida tends to follow the shape of the land more than most homeowners expect, sitting higher under a hilltop and closer to the surface in a low swale nearby, a pattern researchers have documented in Orange County and that shows up across the state's flat terrain generally. A long or sloped driveway in {city} can cross both conditions in the same pour, which means the subgrade at one end may need different treatment than the other. Treating the whole run as a single soil story is how a low corner gets missed, the one spot that actually needed extra compacted fill before the forms went up.</p>"),
  ],
  "build": [
    ("Florida's Minimum Isn't What We Build a Driveway To",
     "<p>Florida's residential code sets 3 1/2 inches as the minimum thickness for a slab on the ground, but that number is written for ordinary floors, not a surface that takes a car's full weight every day. For daily passenger traffic, we build a {city} driveway a full 4 inches thick, poured at a strength in the 3,000 to 4,000 psi range. The extra half inch over the code minimum is not padding: it gives the slab room to carry point loads, like a jack stand or a trailer tongue, without stressing the edge of what code treats as acceptable. Garage floors and carports fall under the same code section but carry their own strength requirement by weathering exposure.</p>"),
    ("Why a 4-Inch Slab Isn't Enough for a Boat Trailer",
     "<p>A standard 4-inch driveway is built for cars and light trucks, not for an RV, a boat trailer or anything that concentrates weight on a narrow set of wheels. For that kind of load, we take the slab to 5 or 6 inches thick, which spreads the weight over more concrete and resists the point-load cracking a thinner slab would show first. Anyone in {city} planning to park something heavier than a daily driver should say so before the forms go in, since thickening a finished driveway later means tearing it out. Mix strength matters too, but added thickness does more for a point load than extra psi alone.</p>"),
    ("Control Joints Aren't Decoration, They're Where We Want It to Crack",
     "<p>Concrete cracks as it shrinks while curing, and a control joint is cut into the slab specifically to decide where that crack happens instead of leaving it to chance. Industry guidance puts joint spacing at roughly 24 to 36 times the slab's thickness, which works out to about 8 to 12 feet apart on a standard 4-inch driveway, and never past about 15 feet even on a wider {city} pour. Each panel should come out close to square rather than long and narrow, and the groove itself needs to reach at least a quarter of the slab's depth to actually control the crack instead of just marking the surface.</p>"),
    ("Timing the Saw Cut Changes What the Joint Looks Like",
     "<p>A control joint cut too late can miss the crack it was meant to control, and one cut too early can tear the surface instead of slicing it cleanly. Early-entry saws go in about 1 to 4 hours after finishing, while a conventional wet saw waits 4 to 12 hours until the concrete has firmed up enough to cut without ravelling the edge. The right window shifts with temperature and humidity on a given {city} pour day, which is why we watch the slab rather than work off a fixed clock. Getting this step wrong shows up later as a ragged joint edge or a crack that wandered off the cut line.</p>"),
    ("What Wire Mesh Actually Does in a Driveway Slab",
     "<p>Wire mesh in a driveway slab does not stop concrete from cracking, and no reinforcement at this thickness does. What it does is hold a crack together once it forms, keeping the two sides from separating or lifting unevenly under a tire. That distinction matters because a {city} homeowner who assumes mesh means a crack-free driveway is set up to be disappointed by a hairline crack that shows up anyway, even though the slab is doing exactly what it was designed to do. The joints still handle where cracks happen; the mesh is backup for whatever forms between them.</p>"),
    ("The Plastic Sheet Most People Never See Under a Slab",
     "<p>Code calls for a 6-mil polyethylene vapor retarder under most slabs on the ground, lapped at least 6 inches at the seams, before the concrete goes down. It is one of the few parts of a driveway build nobody sees again once the pour is finished, but it keeps ground moisture from migrating up through the slab over time. Garages and unheated accessory structures get some exceptions to this rule, so what is required under a {city} driveway next to an attached garage can differ from what is required under a detached slab a few feet away. It is a small material cost for a step that is hard to add back in later.</p>"),
    ("Choosing a Finish Before the Truck Shows Up",
     "<p>A broom finish is the plain, standard surface most driveways get: a broom is dragged across the concrete while it is still workable, leaving a texture that sheds water and resists slipping. Exposed aggregate goes a step further, washing or blasting the surface cement off so the stone inside the mix shows through, which changes both the look and the grip underfoot. Both sit within the basic tier of finish work, well short of stamped or multi-color decorative treatments. The choice has to be made before the pour starts in {city}, since a broom finish cannot become exposed aggregate after the fact without resurfacing.</p>"),
    ("Why an L-Shaped Driveway Panel Is a Problem Before It's Poured",
     "<p>Joint layout is not just about spacing, it is also about shape. Guidance on control joints calls for panels that come out close to square, with the length no more than about one and a half times the width, and specifically warns against L-shaped panels. A driveway in {city} that wraps around a porch step or a utility pad often tempts a layout with exactly that shape, and an L-shaped section concentrates stress at the inside corner no matter how well the rest of the slab is built. We plan extra joints into that corner at layout time instead of hoping the shape holds on its own.</p>"),
  ],
  "process": [
    ("What Actually Happens Before the Concrete Truck Arrives",
     "<p>Forming and grading come before anything gets poured. We set the forms to the final layout, grade and compact the subgrade, and if {city} requires a permit for the work, the inspector typically needs to see the forms and base before concrete covers them up. Only after that review, where one applies, do we schedule the truck. Skipping straight to a pour date without this sequence is how a driveway ends up with a base nobody checked, which is the most common point where later problems start. The forming and grading day usually takes longer than the pour itself.</p>"),
    ("Why We Start Early on a Summer Pour",
     "<p>On a hot {city} morning, waiting until midday to pour concrete works against a crew instead of for it. Guidance for hot-weather concreting calls for avoiding the hottest part of the day, because high heat speeds up how fast the surface dries relative to how fast the slab underneath is gaining strength. We start early so there is more working time before evaporation becomes a problem, and so finishing and the start of curing both happen before the afternoon heat peaks. Starting late is one of the easiest mistakes to avoid and still one of the most common.</p>"),
    ("Curing Starts the Moment the Finish Work Ends",
     "<p>The pour is the visible part of the job, but curing is where the slab actually gains its strength. Once finishing is done, the surface needs to stay moist, whether through a curing compound or wet curing, for at least 3 days, and a white-pigmented compound helps by reflecting heat rather than absorbing it. A {city} driveway poured in summer loses moisture faster than one poured in a mild week, so we adjust the curing method to the weather instead of using the same approach year-round. Rushing this step does not save any real time and is the fastest way to weaken a slab that looked fine on pour day.</p>"),
    ("Working Around Afternoon Storms Instead of Through Them",
     "<p>Central Florida's rainy season typically runs from late May into October, with afternoon storms that build fast on a summer day. Scheduling a {city} pour during that stretch, we plan around the forecast rather than hoping the rain holds off, because fresh concrete caught in a downpour before it sets can wash out the surface finish or dilute the top layer. Morning pours give the slab more time to firm up before a typical afternoon storm arrives. Outside the rainy season, the schedule has more flexibility, but we watch the forecast the same way on every job regardless.</p>"),
    ("The Paperwork That Happens Before Anyone Touches a Shovel",
     "<p>On a job with a direct contract over $2,500, Florida law requires a Notice of Commencement recorded and posted before work actually starts, with permit cards carrying a warning that skipping this step can mean paying twice. For a {city} homeowner, part of the process happens at a desk, not on the driveway: paperwork gets filed and the notice gets posted at the job site before the crew mobilizes. It has nothing to do with concrete chemistry and everything to do with protecting the homeowner's right to the property if a dispute ever comes up later.</p>"),
    ("Demolition Day Looks Nothing Like Pour Day",
     "<p>Replacing an existing driveway starts with tearing out what is there, breaking up and hauling off the old slab before any new base work begins. That is a separate phase from forming, grading and pouring, and on a {city} job it usually happens on its own day or two rather than the same day as the new pour. Once the old concrete is gone, we can finally see and test the subgrade underneath it, which sometimes changes the plan if the soil under a decades-old slab turns out wetter or weaker than expected. Skipping ahead to forming before demolition is fully cleared is how schedules slip.</p>"),
    ("Why the Saw Comes Out Hours, Not Days, After the Pour",
     "<p>Joint cutting happens on a tight clock relative to the pour, not on a separate visit days later. Depending on the saw and the weather, cuts go in anywhere from about 1 hour to about 12 hours after finishing, timed to when the concrete has firmed up enough to cut cleanly without the edge crumbling. On a {city} job, we track the slab's set time rather than work from a fixed schedule, since a hot, dry afternoon firms concrete up faster than an overcast one. Missing that window in either direction is how a joint ends up ragged or badly placed relative to where the slab actually wants to crack.</p>"),
    ("What a Permit Inspection Actually Checks Before the Pour",
     "<p>Where {city} or the county requires a permit for the work, an inspector typically looks at the job before concrete gets poured, not after, checking things like form layout, base material and any reinforcement going in. That review happens at the forming stage, which is one more reason forming and base work take real time rather than getting rushed. A failed or delayed inspection pushes the pour date back, so we schedule the inspection early in the process to protect the rest of the timeline. Jurisdictions differ in exactly what gets checked, so confirming the local process before we mobilize avoids a surprise delay.</p>"),
  ],
  "risks": [
    ("The Cracking That Starts Before the Slab Even Cures",
     "<p>When the evaporation rate from a fresh slab climbs above roughly 0.2 pounds per square foot per hour, which happens on hot, windy, low-humidity days, the surface can dry out faster than the concrete underneath gains strength. The result is plastic shrinkage cracking: thin, usually parallel cracks spaced a few feet apart that show up within hours of finishing. A {city} pour on a hot, breezy afternoon is exactly the kind of day this happens on, and once those cracks form they cannot be undone, only patched. Windbreaks, fog sprays and a dampened subgrade all reduce the risk, but only when the crew is watching the conditions, not just the clock.</p>"),
    ("Sprinkling Water on a Slab to Finish It Faster Backfires",
     "<p>When concrete starts to stiffen before finishing is done, sprinkling water on the surface is a tempting shortcut and a documented bad one: hot-weather guidance specifically warns against it, because it weakens the top layer of the slab exactly where it needs to be strongest. The same caution applies to adding extra water to a load that is already more than an hour and a half old just to make it easier to work. On a hot {city} pour, we manage the pace of the work itself rather than fix a stiffening slab with a hose, since the surface that results from sprinkling is softer and less durable than it looks.</p>"),
    ("A Driveway Can Lift Years After the Tree Was Already There",
     "<p>Live oak roots keep growing long after a driveway is poured, and a root that was not a problem at install can lift a slab edge five or ten years later. Orlando, Winter Park and Sarasota all protect larger trees by ordinance, and cutting into a protected root system without a permit can be a code violation even if the tree itself looks like it is just dropping leaves on the driveway. A {city} homeowner who notices a slab edge starting to tent near a mature tree is better off checking the local tree ordinance before calling anyone with a saw, since the fix has to account for the tree's status, not just the concrete.</p>"),
    ("A Settling Driveway and a Sinkhole Aren't the Same Thing",
     "<p>Most cracked or uneven driveways are settlement, not sinkholes: a sign the base underneath was not compacted enough or that soil beneath the fill shifted over time. A true catastrophic ground cover collapse under Florida law involves a sudden, visible depression and structural damage, not a slowly widening crack at a control joint. Orange County sits on the state's broader list of counties with concentrated sinkhole claims; Sarasota and Manatee do not. Either way, a {city} homeowner seeing gradual unevenness in a driveway is almost always looking at a compaction or drainage issue a contractor can diagnose, not a geological event that needs reporting to the state.</p>"),
    ("What Salt Air Does to a Driveway Near the Coast",
     "<p>A driveway close enough to the Gulf for salt air to reach it ages differently than one further inland: airborne salt speeds up corrosion of any exposed rebar or wire mesh at a crack or an edge, which can widen that crack faster than plain weathering would on its own. Coastal stretches of Sarasota and Manatee also carry FEMA AE and VE flood zone designations in places, which affects more than the driveway but is worth knowing before planning concrete work there. If {city} sits in one of those coastal pockets, it is worth asking how that factors into the reinforcement and finish choices for the job.</p>"),
    ("Irrigation Heads Aimed at the Driveway Cause Slow Damage",
     "<p>A sprinkler head that has drifted even slightly over the years can end up soaking the edge of a driveway every time the zone runs, and that repeated wetting works against a compacted base the same way standing rainwater would, just more predictably. It also leaves mineral staining on the concrete surface that gets worse with every cycle. On a {city} lot, it is worth checking irrigation head aim against the new driveway's footprint before the first watering cycle after the pour, since a head that was fine aimed at an old garden bed is not necessarily fine aimed at fresh concrete and a compacted edge that has not fully settled yet.</p>"),
    ("No License Required Doesn't Mean No Rules Apply",
     "<p>Florida law bars local governments from requiring a license specifically for driveway installation, but that exemption does not do away with permits, and a {city} jurisdiction can still require one for the work. It is narrower than it sounds at first. Separately, as of the 2026 statutes, an HOA cannot require a building permit to already be issued before it will even review a project, which changes the order some homeowners assume these steps happen in. Treating a license exemption as blanket permission is how a driveway project runs into a stop-work order or an HOA dispute that proper sequencing would have avoided.</p>"),
    ("Oversized Panels Crack Wherever They Want To",
     "<p>A driveway poured without enough control joints, or with panels larger than roughly 8 to 12 feet across on a standard 4-inch slab, does not crack less, it just cracks somewhere nobody chose. The same risk shows up when a layout wraps the concrete around a porch step or utility pad into an L shape instead of planning extra joints at that corner. A {city} driveway with either problem usually looks fine for the first year, then develops a crack that runs diagonally across a panel instead of along a joint line, which is both a weaker crack and a worse-looking one than the joints were designed to produce.</p>"),
  ],
  "care": [
    ("Reading Rainy Season Numbers Before the Storms Start",
     "<p>Orlando averages about 51.45 inches of rain a year and Sarasota-Bradenton about 49.05, most of it concentrated in a rainy season that typically runs from late May into October. Before that season starts, it is worth walking a {city} driveway and clearing leaves, mulch and sand out of the control joints, since a joint packed with debris cannot channel water the way an open one does. Standing water that collects against a slab edge because a joint is clogged works on the base underneath over an entire wet season, not just one storm, which is a slower version of the same settlement problem poor compaction causes.</p>"),
    ("Checking Irrigation Timing Against a New Driveway",
     "<p>On the Orlando side of the service area, the year-round rule caps each irrigation zone at about an hour, with up to three-quarters of an inch of water, run on specific days by address, while the Sarasota and Manatee side is currently under a stricter once-a-week schedule tied to the last digit of the address. Either way, it is worth confirming a {city} driveway's edge sits outside a zone's spray pattern once the new restrictions and the new concrete are both in place, since a head that barely clears an old garden bed may land differently relative to a driveway that was not there before. A quick adjustment after the schedule changes costs nothing and saves the slab years of overspray.</p>"),
    ("Why the Groove in a Control Joint Needs to Stay Clear",
     "<p>The groove cut into a driveway's control joints has to reach at least a quarter of the slab's thickness to do its job of controlling where the concrete cracks, and that same depth is what lets sand, dirt and organic debris pack into the joint over time if nobody checks it. On a {city} driveway, keeping those grooves swept out, especially heading into the rainy season, keeps them working the way they were cut to work instead of silting in and trapping water against the edges of each panel. It is a five-minute task with a broom, not a specialty job, and it is easy to let slide for years at a time.</p>"),
    ("Watching for Root Heave Before It Becomes a Crack",
     "<p>A slab edge that starts to tent or lift slightly near a mature oak is an early sign of root growth underneath, worth noticing before it turns into a full crack. Orlando and Winter Park both regulate trees at 6 inches in diameter by ordinance, so the right first move in a {city} yard with a protected tree nearby is checking the local rule, not reaching for a shovel or a saw. Catching heave early sometimes lets a contractor address the slab section without disturbing the root system at all, where waiting until the crack is severe narrows the options considerably.</p>"),
    ("Telling Normal Cracking From Something Worth a Call",
     "<p>A hairline crack along or near a control joint, especially in a driveway with large panels or a few years on it, is typically ordinary shrinkage and settlement, not a structural problem. What is worth a call is a sudden, visible depression, especially paired with cracking that reaches a structure rather than just the driveway surface, since that combination is closer to what Florida law treats as a catastrophic ground cover collapse. For a {city} homeowner, the practical rule of thumb is that gradual change over months is normal monitoring, while anything abrupt is worth a quick look rather than waiting for the next routine check.</p>"),
    ("Pressure Washing Without Driving the Sand Out of the Joints",
     "<p>Pressure washing is a normal part of keeping a driveway's finish looking like it did on pour day, and sizing the job is straightforward: a single-car driveway runs roughly 200 to 400 square feet, a two-car 400 to 700, and anything larger climbs from there. What is easy to overlook on a {city} driveway is that a wand aimed straight down a control joint can blast out sand or stabilizer that was doing a job, leaving the joint more open to weeds and debris than it was before. Angling the spray across the joint rather than into it keeps the cleaning from undoing maintenance that was already done.</p>"),
    ("What Coastal Salt Air Means for Long-Term Upkeep",
     "<p>A driveway in reach of salt air, which applies to parts of coastal Sarasota and Manatee more than it does to the Orlando side of the service area, benefits from an occasional close look at any exposed rebar or wire mesh near a crack or a chipped edge, since airborne salt speeds up corrosion once metal is exposed to it. In a flood-prone coastal pocket in {city}, that inspection matters more than it would further inland, simply because the exposure is more constant. Catching a small chip before salt air reaches the reinforcement underneath costs far less than dealing with a corroded edge later.</p>"),
    ("A Driveway on Flatwoods Soil Needs Drainage Checked Every Wet Season",
     "<p>Flatwoods soils like Myakka and Smyrna hold water close to the surface for months at a stretch during the wetter part of the year, which means a driveway built on that kind of lot depends on its original grading continuing to shed water the way it was designed to. Mulch beds, new landscaping or a few years of settling can quietly change how water moves across a {city} yard and start directing runoff back toward the slab instead of away from it. Checking that the grading still slopes the right way each time the yard changes is cheap insurance against the kind of slow saturation that got the base into trouble in the first place.</p>"),
  ],
}
